course: dictionary
lesson: 4267
title: Graveyard: one word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues. Ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: The first meaning is a piece of land, often around a church, where dead people are buried. «Thousands of wild boar live in and around Berlin, digging up parks, gardens and graveyards for roots and grubs» is about a place on the ground, not a spooky place at night.
explain: The second meaning is a place where old, broken or unwanted things are left, as in «a car graveyard» or «an elephant graveyard». The third meaning is slang: «graveyard shift» means work done late at night until morning. If the word «shift» is nearby, think about time, not burial.
explain: To check, put your meaning back into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A graveyard is a place where dead people are buried, often next to a church.
fact: «Graveyard» can also mean a place where old, broken or unwanted things are left, such as «a car graveyard».
fact: «Graveyard shift» is informal slang for work done late at night until morning.
fact: Gravestones in a graveyard can tell us the names and ages of people from the past.
fact: Real graveyards are calm, respectful places, although spooky stories often show them at night.
fact: «The elephant graveyard» is a myth: there is no secret place where elephants go to die.
exercise: «Thousands of wild boar dig up parks, gardens and graveyards in Berlin for roots and grubs.» What is a graveyard in this sentence? | Lions rest for twenty hours a day | A shop that sells roots | *A place where dead people are buried | A kind of wild boar
exercise: Sam's dad works the «graveyard shift» at the hospital. What does graveyard shift mean? | A shift that starts at dawn with a picnic | *Work done late at night until morning | Digging graves all day in a churchyard | A holiday spent next to a church
exercise: «The old cars were piled high in the car graveyard.» What does graveyard mean here? | A happy place for picnics | A quiet field where people are buried | *A place where old, broken things are left | A church with a tall tower
test: «There is no real elephant graveyard; elephants die wherever they are.» What does this sentence tell us? | Elephants never die | *Elephants do not have a special secret place to go to die | Elephants only live in Berlin | Elephants have a graveyard where they all go to die
test: The word «churchyard» tells us what about a graveyard? | It is a shop that sells bread | *It is a graveyard beside a church | It is a church with no roof | It is a car park for lorries
exam: Which meaning fits «a car graveyard»? | A late night shift | A place for old, broken or unwanted things | *A place where old, broken or unwanted things are left | A church with a tall tower
